---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import HeadingAnchors from '../components/HeadingAnchors.astro';
import CredentialStorageDiagram from '../components/CredentialStorageDiagram.astro';
import CredentialPrecedenceDiagram from '../components/CredentialPrecedenceDiagram.astro';
import GitAuthModesDiagram from '../components/GitAuthModesDiagram.astro';

/**
 * Credentials — hand-written page (not generated), like ci.astro. Explains, with
 * diagrams, where pncli keeps tokens (plaintext config vs OS keychain), which
 * value wins, and how git gets marketplace credentials (helper vs keychain mode).
 *
 * Keep it consistent with skills/pncli/skills-guide.md ("Keeping credentials in
 * the OS keychain", "Private repos and auth") and skills/pncli/marketplace.md —
 * those are the command references; this page is the picture.
 *
 * Hostnames follow the repo's placeholder rules (`<service>.imagile.dev`).
 */
---

<Base
  title="Credentials — pncli"
  description="Where pncli keeps your tokens — plaintext config or the OS keychain — which value wins, and how git gets marketplace credentials in helper or keychain mode."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-10">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-gold mb-2">Docs</p>
      <h1 class="font-display headline-shadow text-4xl font-black text-ink tracking-tight mb-3">Credentials</h1>
      <p class="text-ink/60">
        pncli authenticates with tokens you generate once and hand over. This page shows where those tokens
        rest under each option, which one wins when several are set, and how git gets a marketplace's
        credential. Every option is reversible, and none changes how commands behave.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-neutral max-w-none
      prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-ink prose-headings:font-black
      prose-h2:text-2xl prose-h2:mt-10 prose-h2:mb-4
      prose-h3:text-lg prose-h3:mt-8 prose-h3:mb-3
      prose-p:text-ink/70
      prose-li:text-ink/70 prose-li:text-sm prose-li:leading-relaxed
      prose-strong:text-ink
      prose-a:text-gold prose-a:font-semibold prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline
      prose-code:font-mono prose-code:text-sm prose-code:text-ink prose-code:bg-ink/5 prose-code:px-1.5 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:rounded
      prose-pre:bg-[#1e1e20] prose-pre:text-[#f2ead9] prose-pre:rounded-xl">

      <h2 id="where-tokens-live">Where pncli keeps your tokens</h2>
      <p>
        There are two storage options. The default, <strong>A</strong>, writes each token into
        <code>~/.pncli/config.json</code> as plaintext — simple, and fine on a machine only you use.
        <strong>B</strong> moves the secret into your operating system's credential store and leaves only
        a reference like <code>keychain:github.token</code> in the file.
      </p>

      <CredentialStorageDiagram />

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>A · Plaintext config</th><th>B · OS keychain</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Set up</td><td><code>pncli config init</code> or <code>pncli config set</code></td><td><code>pncli config keychain set github.token</code> (prompts)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>In <code>config.json</code></td><td>The token itself</td><td><code>"keychain:github.token"</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who can read it</td><td>Anything that can read your home directory — backups, dotfile sync, other tools</td><td>Your OS login; the OS may ask before another app reads it</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Extra cost</td><td>None</td><td>One lookup per command — on Windows, starting PowerShell (under a second)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Needs</td><td>Nothing</td><td>macOS / Windows built-ins; <code>secret-tool</code> (libsecret-tools) on Linux</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 id="where-to-see-it">Where to see it on your machine</h3>
      <p>Option B entries are ordinary items in your OS store, so you can inspect or delete them with the tools you already have:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>OS</th><th>pncli secrets (option B)</th><th>git-auth keychain mode</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Windows</td>
            <td>Credential Manager → Windows Credentials → generic credential <code>pncli:github.token</code></td>
            <td>Generic credential <code>git:https://host/org/repo</code> (Git Credential Manager)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>macOS</td>
            <td>Keychain Access → login → <code>pncli github.token</code> (service <code>pncli</code>)</td>
            <td>Internet password for the repository's host</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Linux</td>
            <td>Passwords and Keys (Seahorse) → <code>pncli github.token</code> (attribute <code>service=pncli</code>)</td>
            <td>The libsecret or Git Credential Manager item for the host</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 id="moving-between-options">Moving between A and B</h3>
      <pre is:raw><code>pncli config keychain migrate --dry-run            # preview
pncli config keychain migrate                      # A → B, every secret
pncli config keychain migrate github.token         # A → B, one key
pncli config keychain migrate --to config          # B → A, keep entries
pncli config keychain migrate --to config --purge  # B → A, delete entries
pncli config keychain status                       # refs + whether they resolve</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing is ever held in only one place mid-move.</strong> A → B writes each secret, reads it back, and only then rewrites the config. B → A rewrites the config before <code>--purge</code> deletes anything.</li>
        <li>A → B leaves <code>config.json.pre-keychain.bak</code> — a plaintext copy for undo. <code>pncli doctor</code> reminds you until you delete it.</li>
        <li>Marketplace tokens can start in the keychain: <code>pncli skills marketplace add &lt;url&gt; --token &lt;token&gt; --keychain</code>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="which-value-wins">Which value wins</h2>
      <p>
        A token can be set in more than one place. pncli takes the first one it finds, and environment
        variables always sit above anything on disk — which is what keeps CI predictable.
      </p>

      <CredentialPrecedenceDiagram />

      <p>
        See <a href="/pncli/ci/">CI Recipes</a> for the full list of CI fallback variables. A keychain
        reference that cannot be read is treated as <em>not set</em> — pncli never sends the literal
        <code>keychain:…</code> string as a credential — and <code>pncli doctor</code> names it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="git-and-marketplaces">How git gets marketplace credentials</h2>
      <p>
        pncli's own <code>marketplace add</code> and <code>sync</code> hand git the marketplace's token for
        each call, through the environment — never on the command line, never in the clone's
        <code>.git/config</code>. But an agent host that clones a marketplace itself (Claude Code's
        <code>/plugin marketplace add</code>, for instance) runs plain <code>git</code>, which knows nothing
        about pncli. <code>pncli skills git-auth enable</code> bridges that, in one of two modes:
      </p>

      <GitAuthModesDiagram />

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>A · Helper mode (default)</th><th>B · Keychain mode</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>What is written</td><td>A <code>credential.&lt;repo-url&gt;.helper</code> entry in your global gitconfig</td><td>A copy of the token in git's credential store, plus a repo-scoped <code>useHttpPath</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Token rotation</td><td>Nothing to do — git always gets pncli's current token</td><td>Re-run <code>enable --mode keychain</code>; doctor flags a stale copy</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Needs pncli on git's PATH</td><td>Yes</td><td>No — works for GUI clients and containers</td></tr>
          <tr><td>If pncli can't answer</td><td>git falls through to your own helpers (<code>gh</code>, Git Credential Manager)</td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Undo</td><td colspan="2"><code>pncli skills git-auth disable</code> restores exactly what was there before</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>A helper-mode entry looks like this — scoped to one repository, so nothing else on the host changes:</p>
      <pre is:raw><code>[credential "https://bitbucket.imagile.dev/scm/ai/skills.git"]
	helper =
	helper = !pncli skills git-credential --marketplace 'internal-ai'
	helper = manager</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>The empty <code>helper =</code> makes pncli the first helper asked; the helper after it (here Git Credential Manager) is whatever you used before, kept as the fallback.</li>
        <li>Each marketplace uses its own token and username: its <code>--token</code>, or the provider token pncli already has — <code>github.token</code>, <code>bitbucket.pat</code>, <code>ado.pat</code>.</li>
        <li>The two options combine: helper mode plus option B means git asks pncli, pncli reads the OS keychain, and the token never sits in a file anywhere.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="checking-what-git-sends">Checking what git actually sends</h2>
      <p>
        git has many places a credential can come from — <code>insteadOf</code> rewrites with a token in them,
        a token in a remote URL, Git Credential Manager, the <code>gh</code> CLI, the Windows Credential
        Manager. These commands show which one wins, never printing more than a fingerprint like
        <code>ghp_…a1b2</code>:
      </p>
      <pre is:raw><code># every remote of this clone, and whether it can read the repo
pncli git credentials inspect
# every clone under a folder that is unmapped or lacks access
pncli git credentials inspect --scan ~/src --problems-only
# insteadOf rewrites and URL-scoped helpers
pncli git credentials mappings
# what the credential stores hold, and what git actually sends
pncli git credentials stored
# drop a stale stored credential
pncli git credentials forget --host github.com --username octo
# per marketplace: mode, and whether git sends pncli's token
pncli skills git-auth status
# all of the above, with a fix for each problem
pncli doctor</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>stored</code> also flags a <strong>shadowed</strong> credential — one sitting in a store that git
        never sends because another helper answers first — which is the usual answer to “why is git using the
        wrong account?”.
      </p>

      <p>
        More on the concepts behind skills and marketplaces: <a href="/pncli/skills-guide/">Skills guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <HeadingAnchors levels="h2, h3" />
  </div>
</Base>
